# Oh My Zsh plugins, compiled into ~/.cache/zsh/plugins.zsh by antidote.
# Loaded by plugins:load in .functions, which .zshrc calls just before .aliases.
#
# This file is plain (no .tmpl), so chezmoi symlinks it rather than rendering a
# copy. Editing ~/.plugins edits the tracked file directly, and the next shell
# notices it is newer than the compiled bundle and rebuilds.
#
# Order is load order, and load order is the only thing that settles a name two
# plugins both claim. Four placements below are deliberate rather than tidy:
#
#   dotnet before docker       so `dr` stays `docker container run`
#   rails before react-native  so `rn` stays React Native
#   helm before history        so `h` stays history
#   fzf and globalias last     the only entries that bind keys
#
# .aliases loads after the whole bundle and wins any name it defines itself.
#
# Three entries (codex, vault, ngrok) define no aliases. codex and ngrok cache
# the tool's own `completion zsh` output, and vault hands completion to the
# vault binary through bashcompinit. In each case this is the only completion
# that tool has. None of the three needs a guard, because each returns early
# when its command is missing.
#
# `conditional:have:<x>` guards an entry behind a test. The guard functions live
# in .functions, and antidote compiles each into a plain `if have:<x>; then ...
# fi`, so a guarded plugin costs one test and nothing else. Most guards check a
# tool that is not installed here, so the plugin starts working the day the tool
# arrives. have:tty asks for a real terminal instead.

# ------------------------------- Shell Basics ------------------------------- #

ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh path:plugins/common-aliases
ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh path:plugins/encode64
ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh path:plugins/extract
ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh path:plugins/sudo
ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh path:plugins/colored-man-pages

# --------------------------------- Editors ---------------------------------- #

ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh path:plugins/vscode
ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh path:plugins/emacs

